    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 192112\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n112 PM PST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Active weather will persist as a potent storm system moves\n  through the region today and tonight.\n\n* Cool and dry conditions will ensue in the wake of the system \n  Friday and Saturday.\n\n* The pattern remains active with additional storm systems\n  expected heading into Thanksgiving week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nA powerful upper low continues to affect the western CONUS, \nbringing low elevation rain, mountain snow and gusty winds to much\nof the Desert Southwest. This feature will be the main concern \nover the next 12-18 hours. The primary risks associated with this \nsystem remain heavy mountain snow across the Eastern Sierra as \nwell as the White and Spring Mountains, and gusty \nwest/southwesterly winds from this afternoon through around \nmidnight tonight. Regarding rainfall, totals look to remain \nrather light, though the combination of breezy conditions, cooler \ntemperatures, and showers, could make for rather unpleasant \noutdoor conditions. Over the mountains, accumulating snow \ngenerally looks to be confined to elevations above 6000 ft, where \nsome 6-12 inches total may fall. For the Sierra, even higher snow \ntotals are expected, especially in the higher elevations. In \naddition to the precipitation, gusty southwest winds will spread \nover the region from San Bernardino county through southern Clark \nand Mohave counties. Winds of 15-25 mph can be expected with gusts\n35-50 mph from mid/late afternoon into tonight. This could lead \nto patchy blowing dust near any exposed areas or dry lake beds, \nwhich may reduce visibilities for motorists. Otherwise, gusty \nwinds may prove a challenge for high profile vehicles. The system \nwill gradually shift eastward late tonight, bringing a west to \neast end to precipitation overnight, though rain may linger across\nwestern Arizona into the morning hours Friday. Otherwise, cool \nand dry conditions will ensue and winds should gradually subside \novernight.\n\nFor the remainder of the weekend, flow looks to become a bit more\nzonal. This will result in continued breeziness on Friday, though\nnothing worthy of any advisories. Temperatures will remain some \n5-10 degrees below seasonal normals so it will definitely feel \nchilly, especially at night.\n\nThe pattern looks to remain active heading into next week with \ngeneral troughiness across the western U.S. However, considerable \ndifferences exist between deterministic and ensemble guidance \nregarding timing and intensity of any particular system. We will \ncontinue to monitor for any potential impacts to Thanksgiving \nholiday travel.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...\nA fairly active day expected with increasing winds, lowering \nceilings, and scattered showers. Generally light east to southeast \nwinds through 20z, but are forecast to shift more to the west after \n20z; however, winds are still expected to remain less than 7 knots. \nScattered showers are expected through the afternoon with CIGs \ngenerally around 6kft, but may drop to around 5kft in some of the \nheavier showers. It is also unlikely that visibility will drop below \n6SM. After 02Z, expect showers to gradually diminish with clouds \nbecoming FEW-SCT. Starting around 00Z, expect winds to shift and \nincrease out of the southwest with gusts around 20 knots, increasing \nto around 30 knots after 02Z, before gradually diminishing through \nthe evening. Improving conditions for Friday with lighter winds and \nVFR ceilings. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Scattered showers are \nexpected for most TAF sites today with CIGs generally 6-8kft, but \nlowering to around 4-5kft in heavier showers. Showers will diminish \nlate this afternoon with CIGs about 7kft, gradually clearing \novernight. At KBIH, expected CIGs generally between 3-4kft with more \nwidespread rain showers for much of the afternoon with occasional \nvisibility reductions to 5SM. Winds at KBIH will be southeast with \ngusts to around 30 knots, but drop to around 12 knots after sunset, \nbefore becoming light and variable later this evening. Elsewhere, \nwinds generally less than 10 knots through mid-afternoon, increasing \nout of the southwest to west 15-25 knots with gusts to around 30 \nknots into this evening before diminishing. Much quieter conditions \nexpected the rest of the day on Friday. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...\nAVIATION...Gorelow\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
